scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Use a dict keyed by the immutable id, assigning the first-seen record and ignoring later ones. Dict preserves insertion order, so the first binding wins if you guard against overwrites.

```python
seen = {}
for e in events:
    if e["id"] not in seen:
        seen[e["id"]] = e  # keep first occurrence

unique_events = list(seen.values())
```

This avoids hashing unhashable nested structures because only the id (a hashable string) is used as a key. It is stable (first occurrence kept) due to dict insertion order. It also makes conflict policy explicit; you could choose last-write-wins by unconditionally assigning instead.

